  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1138 AM MDT Sat May 30 2026\n\nCOOLER TODAY, MORE SEASONABLE TOMORROW:\n\nWe continue to be under the influence of a low pressure system \nthat is currently centered in southwest Wyoming to our north. \nTemperatures will be much cooler today (5-15 degrees below \nnormal for the end of May) with variable cloud cover. Locations \nalong and north of I-70 will see afternoon showers and isolated \nstorms as the low continues to pass by to the north. Wind gusts\nup to 45 mph will be the primary threat of today's convection. \n\nTomorrow, there may be enough lingering moisture for afternoon \nshowers in the northern mountains. Otherwise, conditions dry out\nand warm up by 5 to 10 degrees. \n\nWORK WEEK WARM UP: \n\nTemperatures continue to rise heading into the new week as the \nforecast area remains under southwest flow aloft. This will \npromote a relatively quiet weather pattern on Monday and \nTuesday, before moisture begins to advect into southwest \nColorado from the south. Monday looks to be the driest day this \nweek with single-digit afternoon humidity values for the lower \nvalleys along and south of I- 70. As a result, elevated to \ncritical fire weather conditions are anticipated. Coverage of \ncritical fire weather conditions is best across parts of \nsouthwest Colorado, but remains low enough to hold off on fire \nweather products for the time being. \n\nMoisture advection intensifies on Wednesday leading to \nincreasing chances for afternoon showers and storms over the \nhigher terrain, especially the southern mountains. Afternoon \nprecipitation chances continue over the southern and central \nmountains through the latter half of the week, but coverage and \nQPF will decrease with drier air working back in from the west. \nThere is currently a stronger signal for juicier PWATs in the \nGEFS compared to other global ensemble families. This means the \nforecast could trend drier if the EC ENS et al. win out, or \nwetter if the GEFS is on the right track. Stay tuned!",
